---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import GuideToc from "../../components/GuideToc.astro";
import NoteCard from "../../components/NoteCard.astro";

// The single, manual place to define what shows up in the sidebar/mobile ToC
// for this guide. Top-level entries map to a section's own heading; nested
// `children` map to `<h3>` headings within that section — add/reorder entries
// here to control the ToC without touching the content markup below.
const toc = [
  { id: "about-this-document", label: "About this document" },
  {
    id: "part-one-general",
    label: "Part One: General",
    children: [
      {
        id: "what-is-the-works-with-home-assistant-program",
        label: "What is the Works with Home Assistant Program?",
      },
      { id: "program-requirements", label: "Program Requirements" },
      {
        id: "correct-use-of-home-assistant-and-wwha-badge",
        label: "Correct Use of Home Assistant and WWHA Badge",
      },
      { id: "core-integrations", label: "Core Integrations" },
      { id: "partnership-promise", label: "Partnership Promise" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "part-two-technical-requirements",
    label: "Part Two: Technical Requirements",
    children: [
      { id: "matter", label: "Matter" },
      { id: "zigbee", label: "Zigbee" },
      { id: "z-wave", label: "Z-Wave" },
      { id: "esphome", label: "ESPHome" },
      { id: "bluetooth", label: "Bluetooth" },
      { id: "wi-fi", label: "Wi-Fi" },
      { id: "prohibited-devices", label: "Prohibited Devices" },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="WWHA Compliance Guide"
  description="Compliance and certification requirements for the Works with Home Assistant program."
>
  <div class="wrapper wrapper-overflow-visible">
    <section class="guide-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <h1 id="compliance-guide">Compliance Guide</h1>
        <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="guide">
      <div class="guide-layout container">
        <GuideToc toc={toc} />

        <div class="guide-panels">
          <section class="guide-panel">
            <h2 id="about-this-document">About this document</h2>
            <hr />
            <p>
              This guide outlines the compliance and certification requirements
              for the Works with Home Assistant (WWHA) program. It is intended
              for manufacturers who wish to integrate with Home Assistant and
              certify their devices accordingly.
            </p>
            <p>The document is split into two parts:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="#part-one-general"
                  ><strong>Part One: General</strong></a
                > &mdash; An overview of the program, its purpose, and the requirements
                that apply to all partners.
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="#part-two-technical-requirements"
                  ><strong>Part Two: Technical</strong></a
                > &mdash; Protocol-specific requirements for each certification pathway
                (Matter, Zigbee, Z-Wave, ESPHome, Bluetooth, and Wi-Fi), as well as
                a section on prohibited devices.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Further testing specifics are also available in our <a
                href="https://www.openhomefoundation.org/assets/documents/works-with-home-assistant-device-test-report-10-dec-2025.pdf"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">sample testing document</a
              >, but please note that this is not exhaustive, as testing
              parameters have to be flexible based on device type and behaviour.
            </p>
          </section>

          <hr class="part-divider" />

          <section class="guide-panel">
            <h2 class="part-title" id="part-one-general">Part One: General</h2>

            <h3 id="what-is-the-works-with-home-assistant-program">
              What is the Works with Home Assistant Program?
            </h3>
            <p>
              Home Assistant is an open source platform built to give users
              choice and control over their smart home and data. It is powered
              by a welcoming global community of users and developers, ensuring
              the platform is constantly evolving and improving. By keeping
              everything local and prioritizing privacy, Home Assistant offers a
              simple, powerful, and sustainable way to automate the home now and
              in the future.
            </p>
            <p>
              The WWHA program certifies that a device integrates seamlessly and
              reliably with Home Assistant. When users see the WWHA badge on a
              product, they can trust that it will work as expected &mdash;
              immediately upon installation, locally, and without needing
              specialist technical knowledge.
            </p>
            <p>
              The program is run under the Open Home Foundation. This foundation
              champions the fundamental principles of privacy, choice, and
              sustainability for smart homes and for every person who lives in
              one. We achieve this by supporting the development of open source
              projects and open connectivity standards.
            </p>

            <h3 id="program-requirements">Program Requirements</h3>
            <p>
              All devices entering the WWHA program must meet the following
              general requirements, in addition to any protocol-specific
              requirements outlined in Part Two.
            </p>
            <dl>
              <div>
                <dt>Safety Certification</dt>
                <dd>
                  Products must have relevant safety certification for the
                  region sold and device type: FCC/CE, etc.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Firmware Updates</dt>
                <dd>
                  Firmware updates should ideally be available from within Home
                  Assistant.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Support and Maintenance</dt>
                <dd>
                  Support Contact and Integration Maintainer &mdash; if the
                  integration is not using an open standard such as Matter,
                  Zigbee or Z-Wave, the partner must be the maintainer of their
                  integration and provide the Open Home Foundation with the
                  contact details of the developers in charge. If the
                  integration is via an open standard, we require a technical
                  contact from your team responsible for this.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Certification Agreement</dt>
                <dd>
                  All WWHA partners will need to agree to the terms of <a
                    href="https://www.openhomefoundation.org/assets/documents/works-with-home-assistant-certification-agreement-13-aug-2026.pdf"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer">the certification agreement</a
                  >.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Testing</dt>
                <dd>
                  Devices tested and approved &mdash; two devices per product
                  need to be sent to us for technical and support testing.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Community Standards</dt>
                <dd>Be a positive brand in the Home Assistant community.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Commercial Fee</dt>
                <dd>
                  Commercial fee: 500 Swiss Franc annually (per partner, not per
                  device).
                </dd>
              </div>
            </dl>

            <h3 id="correct-use-of-home-assistant-and-wwha-badge">
              Correct Use of Home Assistant and WWHA Badge
            </h3>
            <p>
              The Home Assistant logo and WWHA Badge are trademarked symbols of
              our mission and commitment to privacy, choice and sustainability.
              As a certified partner, you will be granted access to the WWHA
              badge for use on your products and documentation. This signals
              that your product aligns with our core values and has gone through
              certification. Correct use of the Home Assistant brand and WWHA
              badge is essential to maintaining trust with our shared community.
            </p>
            <p>
              Certified partners who intend to use the Home Assistant logo and
              WWHA badge must adhere to the Open Home Foundation&rsquo;s
              trademarks and guidelines.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you&rsquo;re currently misusing any trademarks of the Open Home
              Foundation, such as the WWHA badge and not adhering to our Brand
              Guidelines, we will not consider your application until the use is
              rectified.
            </p>

            <h3 id="core-integrations">Core Integrations</h3>
            <p>
              Connecting a manufacturer&rsquo;s device to Home Assistant via
              Wi-Fi or Bluetooth requires an integration. While anyone can
              create and distribute a custom integration independently,
              integrations that meet a defined set of quality requirements can
              be built directly into Home Assistant. These are called core
              integrations.
            </p>
            <p>
              Core integrations are used by the majority of the Home Assistant
              community. They are designed to provide a simple, reliable
              experience for users &mdash; immediately upon installation, with
              no additional downloads or manual configuration required. Core
              integrations are built and maintained by community members, the
              core team, and device manufacturers who wish to support their
              devices within the platform.
            </p>
            <p>
              For partners who wish to build or take ownership of a core
              integration, please get in touch with us for a Core Integration
              Summary. Technical specifics are in Part Two.
            </p>

            <h3 id="partnership-promise">Partnership Promise</h3>
            <p>
              In exchange for meeting the certification requirements, certified
              partners receive the following from the Open Home Foundation:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Works With Home Assistant badge &mdash; access to the applicable
                trademarked WWHA badges for use on product packaging and
                documentation pages.
              </li>
              <li>
                Dedicated integration page &mdash; showing the Works With Home
                Assistant logo and noting the partnership. Our two million users
                search these pages to find devices to integrate.
              </li>
              <li>
                Co-marketing &mdash; we will use Home Assistant&rsquo;s Blog and
                Social Media accounts to announce and promote the partnership to
                our users.
              </li>
              <li>
                Certified devices will appear on a <a
                  href="/certified-products/">published, searchable list</a
                >.
              </li>
              <li>
                Quarterly updates &mdash; Open Home Foundation will discuss
                upcoming changes and features to the platform, the program, and
                more.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <hr class="part-divider" />

          <section class="guide-panel">
            <h2 class="part-title" id="part-two-technical-requirements">
              Part Two: Technical Requirements
            </h2>
            <p>
              This section details the technical requirements for each
              certification pathway. Identify which protocol your devices use
              and ensure you meet the relevant requirements.
            </p>

            <h3 id="matter">Matter</h3>
            <p>
              The Home Assistant Matter Integration is available within Home
              Assistant and is maintained by our team. For the purposes of the
              Works with program, devices are tested against this integration.
            </p>
            <NoteCard noIcon>
              <p>
                Integration link: <a
                  href="https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/matter/"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >home-assistant.io/integrations/matter</a
                >
              </p>
            </NoteCard>

            <h4 id="matter-csa-certification">
              01. Connectivity Standards Alliance (CSA) Certification
            </h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Matter items must be CSA certified.</li>
              <li>
                Products that are not yet released will not be certified with us
                until CSA certification is complete. We can still receive them
                to our team in tandem with the CSA process.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="matter-vendor-specific-clusters">
              02. Vendor-Specific (Custom) Clusters
            </h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                We only accept custom clusters/attributes for functionality that
                is not available in the official Matter spec, nor will be in the
                foreseeable future.
              </li>
              <li>
                If a device contains vendor-specific clusters with relevant
                additional configuration that can be beneficial for users (e.g.
                only controllable by a vendor-specific app), vendors may provide
                the information to us so we can add parsing of these details.
              </li>
              <li>
                We require written documentation including the following
                information:
                <ul>
                  <li>Cluster ID of the custom cluster</li>
                  <li>
                    Attribute-IDs and their details (datatype, read/write flags,
                    meaning, unit)
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Command-IDs and their details (request and response data,
                    functionality)
                  </li>
                  <li>Event-IDs and their details</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="matter-firmware-updates">03. Firmware Updates</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                OTA firmware updates should be available to Home Assistant users
                from within the Home Assistant ecosystem.
              </li>
              <li>
                We use the Matter DCL and our server pulls from it daily. Use
                this to provide us with firmware updates.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="matter-general-testing">04. General Testing</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                A device must be able to handle up to five concurrent
                controllers (multi-admin) at the same time. While part of Matter
                certification, this needs additional testing specifically within
                Home Assistant.
              </li>
              <li>
                Devices must have been checked in-house to ensure they can
                handle up to five concurrent controllers.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 id="zigbee">Zigbee</h3>
            <p>
              The Zigbee integration available in Home Assistant is Zigbee Home
              Automation (ZHA). There is also a popular community alternative
              called Zigbee2MQTT (Z2M). For the purposes of the Works with
              program, devices are tested against ZHA. We would encourage all
              manufacturers to also ensure their devices work well with Z2M, as
              some of our more technical users may prefer this option. ZHA uses
              the Zigpy Library.
            </p>
            <NoteCard noIcon>
              <p>
                Integration link: <a
                  href="https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/zha"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >home-assistant.io/integrations/zha</a
                >
              </p>
            </NoteCard>

            <h4 id="zigbee-csa-certification">01. CSA Certification</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Zigbee items must be CSA certified.</li>
              <li>
                Products that are not yet released will not be certified with us
                until CSA certification is complete. We can still receive them
                to our team in tandem with the CSA process.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="zigbee-quirks">02. Quirks</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Most Zigbee devices have functions that work outside of the
                specification. The ZHA integration adheres very closely to the
                Zigbee spec, and therefore if a device has functions outside of
                this, they will need extra code to be added to our libraries. We
                refer to these Python scripts as &lsquo;Quirks&rsquo;.
              </li>
              <li>
                If you do not have resources in-house to contribute to these
                scripts, we recommend getting in touch with us to reach the
                community and sub-contract these to an individual freelancer.
              </li>
              <li>
                Alternatively, manufacturers must be able to provide us with
                written documentation detailing what each quirk should do and
                any relevant values, so our team can input these quirks
                depending on our own internal resources.
              </li>
              <li>
                Writing &lsquo;quirks&rsquo; in the ZHA libraries for devices is
                the manufacturer&rsquo;s responsibility. You will need the
                internal resources to submit quirks to GitHub.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="zigbee-firmware-updates">03. Firmware Updates</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                OTA firmware updates must be available to Home Assistant users
                from within the Home Assistant ecosystem.
              </li>
              <li>
                Instructions on how to submit: <a
                  href="https://github.com/zigpy/zigpy/wiki/OTA-Information-for-Manufacturers"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >github.com/zigpy/zigpy/wiki/OTA-Information-for-Manufacturers</a
                >
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 id="z-wave">Z-Wave</h3>
            <p>
              A Z-Wave integration is available within Home Assistant which is
              maintained by our team. For the purposes of the Works with
              program, devices are tested against this integration. It uses the
              Z-Wave JS driver (<a
                href="https://zwave-js.github.io/zwave-js/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">zwave-js.github.io/zwave-js</a
              >).
            </p>

            <h4 id="z-wave-alliance-certification">
              01. Z-Wave Alliance Certification
            </h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Z-Wave items must be Z-Wave Alliance certified.</li>
              <li>
                Products that are not yet released will not be certified with us
                until Z-Wave Alliance certification is complete. We can still
                receive them to our team in tandem with the Z-Wave Alliance
                process.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="z-wave-device-configuration-files">
              02. Device Configuration Files
            </h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Configuration files improve the user experience in Home
                Assistant and any other projects using Z-Wave JS. They commonly
                include:
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    Device identification: manufacturer, label, firmware
                    versions.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Associations: to improve what the device reports or to
                    provide info the device does not report.
                  </li>
                  <li>Configuration parameters.</li>
                  <li>
                    Toggling device-specific workarounds for firmware bugs.
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                Guide for contributing configuration files: <a
                  href="https://zwave-js.github.io/zwave-js/#/config-files/contributing-files"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >zwave-js.github.io/zwave-js/#/config-files/contributing-files</a
                >
              </li>
              <li>
                Please conform to the style guide in order to label well for the
                UI.
              </li>
              <li>
                If you do not have resources in-house, we recommend getting in
                touch with us to reach the community and sub-contract to an
                individual freelancer. Alternatively, provide us with written
                documentation so our team can input these files depending on our
                own internal resource.
              </li>
              <li>
                Submitting device config files is the manufacturer&rsquo;s
                responsibility and a requirement of the WWHA program.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="z-wave-firmware-updates">03. Firmware Updates</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                OTA firmware updates should be available to Home Assistant users
                from within the Home Assistant ecosystem.
              </li>
              <li>
                Instructions on how to submit: <a
                  href="https://github.com/zwave-js/firmware-updates/"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >github.com/zwave-js/firmware-updates</a
                >
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 id="esphome">ESPHome</h3>
            <p>
              The ESPHome Integration is available within Home Assistant and
              allows ESPHome devices to connect directly to Home Assistant with
              the native ESPHome API. For the purposes of the Works with
              program, devices are tested against this integration.
            </p>
            <NoteCard noIcon>
              <p>
                Integration link: <a
                  href="https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/esphome/"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >home-assistant.io/integrations/esphome</a
                >
              </p>
            </NoteCard>

            <h4 id="esphome-fcc-ce-certification">
              01. FCC / CE Certification
            </h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                As standard, all items received for testing need the relevant
                safety certification for the region in which they are being
                sold.
              </li>
              <li>
                ESPHome devices are popular with start-up companies who might
                not have experience of this yet &mdash; please confirm you have
                the relevant certification.
              </li>
              <li>Items must be CE certified and/or FCC certified.</li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="esphome-made-for-esphome">02. Made for ESPHome</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Before entering the Works with program, items must first be
                accepted into the <a
                  href="https://devices.esphome.io/made-for-esphome"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer">Made for ESPHome program</a
                >.
              </li>
              <li>
                Current Made for ESPHome requirements include:
                <ul>
                  <li>Devices use an ESP32 chip.</li>
                  <li>
                    Manufacturers must be proactive in keeping their published
                    configuration up to date with the latest ESPHome changes.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    (Optional but recommended) Ideally devices have a USB port
                    which allows users to install firmware and retrieve logs.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    OTA (Over The Air) Requirements:
                    <ul>
                      <li>
                        Must provide pre-built firmware using the update/OTA
                        components in ESPHome.
                      </li>
                      <li>
                        Must provide the configuration source so users can take
                        control and update locally and make modifications
                        themselves.
                      </li>
                    </ul>
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Improv Requirements:
                    <ul>
                      <li>
                        Improv Serial is configured (if USB port accessible).
                      </li>
                      <li>
                        Improv via BLE (Bluetooth Low Energy) is configured.
                        Ideally Improv via BLE requires user presence via the
                        authoriser configuration.
                      </li>
                    </ul>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="esphome-web-flashers">03. Web Flashers</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Web flashing using <a
                  href="https://esphome.github.io/esp-web-tools/"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer">esp-web-tools</a
                > deployed to a public website (e.g. GitHub Pages) allows users to
                install firmware to a device that is not updating via OTA, or where
                the user has previously taken control and wants to revert to provided
                firmware.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="esphome-sensible-sensors">04. Sensible Sensors</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                ESPHome devices can easily provide many sensors, but to most
                users, there is a smaller set of data that is actually relevant
                for their device.
              </li>
              <li>
                Sensors/entities should use sensible filters to limit the
                frequency of data being transferred into Home Assistant.
              </li>
              <li>
                Any sensors that are not primary information that the majority
                of users would want on their dashboard should be marked with the
                entity category &lsquo;diagnostic&rsquo;.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 id="bluetooth">Bluetooth</h3>
            <p>
              A Bluetooth certificate is issued when the Certified partner
              proves that the device or product meets the following
              requirements:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                The device or product is certified by the Bluetooth Special
                Interest Group (Bluetooth SIG); and
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="bluetooth-integration-quality">Integration Quality</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Integration must be set up via the user interface &mdash; users
                do not enter manual YAML code.
              </li>
              <li>
                Integration uses account linking for OAuth &mdash; users do not
                enter passwords or keys directly.
              </li>
              <li>
                Integration has Auto-Discovery, if possible &mdash; users will
                see their devices automatically and can initiate setup
                immediately.
              </li>
              <li>
                An Integration Quality Scale level of Gold or above is required.
              </li>
              <li>
                See <a
                  href="https://developers.home-assistant.io/docs/core/integration-quality-scale/"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >developers.home-assistant.io/docs/core/integration-quality-scale</a
                >
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 id="wi-fi">Wi-Fi</h3>

            <h4 id="wifi-integration-quality">Integration Quality</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Wi-Fi device supports 2.4 GHz as standard.</li>
              <li>
                Integration must be set up via the user interface &mdash; users
                do not enter manual YAML code.
              </li>
              <li>
                Integration uses account linking for OAuth &mdash; users do not
                enter passwords or keys directly.
              </li>
              <li>
                Integration has Auto-Discovery, if possible &mdash; users will
                see their devices automatically and can initiate setup
                immediately.
              </li>
              <li>
                An Integration Quality Scale level of Gold or above is required.
              </li>
              <li>
                See <a
                  href="https://developers.home-assistant.io/docs/core/integration-quality-scale/"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >developers.home-assistant.io/docs/core/integration-quality-scale</a
                >
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 id="prohibited-devices">Prohibited Devices</h3>
            <p>
              The following categories of devices are not eligible for Works
              with Home Assistant certification. If unsure whether a product
              falls into one of these categories, please contact us before
              submitting.
            </p>

            <h4 id="cloud-dependent-devices">Cloud-Dependent Devices</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Devices that do not work locally.</li>
              <li>
                Devices that have a cloud dependency &mdash; i.e. the device
                operates locally but only after Matter or another protocol is
                enabled through a cloud app or similar service.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="connecting-devices">Connecting Devices</h4>
            <h5 id="connecting-devices-definition">Definition</h5>
            <p>
              A connecting device &mdash; e.g. a bridge, hub, stick, repeater,
              antenna, coordinator, or proxy &mdash; is a product whose primary
              function is to help connect other devices to a network or
              protocol, rather than perform a discrete function of its own for
              the end user. Examples:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Joining Zigbee, Z-Wave, or Bluetooth, DALI or KNX devices to
                Wi-Fi, Ethernet, or Matter
              </li>
              <li>Acting as a Thread border router</li>
              <li>
                Multi-protocol coordinators that also run local automation,
                since bridging is still their primary function
              </li>
            </ul>
            <h5 id="connecting-devices-eligibility">Eligibility</h5>
            <p>
              A standalone consumer device (speaker, sensor, lock, etc.)
              doesn&rsquo;t lose eligibility just for having secondary
              connecting capability &mdash; eligibility follows the primary
              advertised function.
            </p>

            <h4 id="products-not-available-to-the-general-public">
              Products Not Available to the General Public
            </h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Items that are not available to purchase by the general public
                &mdash; i.e. only available via an installer.
              </li>
              <li>
                Items that are not intended for the general homeowner &mdash;
                i.e. products that are too installer-focused or too complex for
                a normal user.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="remote-controls">Remote Controls</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Remote controls are not eligible for certification.</li>
              <li>
                Note: The distinction between a remote control and a collection
                of switches (e.g. a wall panel) is an area of ongoing
                discussion.
              </li>
              <li>
                Wall panels running Android or similar operating systems:
                eligibility will be assessed on a case-by-case basis depending
                on whether they fit within the WWHA framework.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="screens-displays">Screens / Displays</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Screens, tablets, e-ink devices and displays for use as a Home
                Assistant dashboard are not eligible for certification.
              </li>
              <li>
                Exceptions include very small screens that are not intended for
                use as a dashboard i.e. a small temperature sensor or air
                quality monitor which has a screen to show the temperature.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="community-built-integrations">
              Community-built Integrations
            </h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Items that use an integration that the manufacturer is not
                responsible for, and that is not an integration for an open
                protocol (such as Zigbee, Z-Wave, or Matter) that is managed by
                the Ecosystem team at the foundation. Examples include WLED,
                Modbus, and MQTT (see MQTT section below).
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="mqtt">MQTT</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                If a device relies on <a
                  href="https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/mqtt/"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >Home Assistant&rsquo;s MQTT broker</a
                >, we do not generally consider this to be operating immediately
                upon installation with Home Assistant.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h5 id="mqtt-context">Context</h5>
            <ul>
              <li>
                MQTT requires the user to install and configure a separate MQTT
                broker. This step is additional and more complex than a standard
                Home Assistant setup.
              </li>
              <li>
                Because the MQTT integration is community-contributed, we do not
                promote or certify individual manufacturer implementations that
                depend on it as they do not fulfill the contractual obligation
                of maintaining your own integration. We cannot guarantee that
                the community-contributed integrations will always function as
                expected or stay maintained.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h5 id="mqtt-alternative-paths-to-certification">
              Alternative Paths to Certification
            </h5>

            <h6 id="mqtt-esphome">ESPHome</h6>
            <p>
              If your device is built on a supported microcontroller chip,
              ESPHome may offer a simpler path to certification. Rather than
              relying on MQTT, ESPHome uses its own native API to communicate
              directly with Home Assistant. This is a more powerful and seamless
              connection that requires no broker or complex setup from the user.
            </p>
            <NoteCard noIcon>
              <p>
                For more information see the ESPHome section above, or visit <a
                  href="https://esphome.io/components/api/"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer">esphome.io/components/api</a
                >.
              </p>
            </NoteCard>

            <h6 id="mqtt-core-integration">Core Integration</h6>
            <p>
              Alternatively, manufacturers who wish to apply to the Works with
              Home Assistant program will need to build a dedicated core
              integration with the broker functionality embedded as part of the
              integration architecture. This will allow the integration to
              connect directly to the broker automatically, for a more seamless
              experience for the end user. See the Core Integrations section in
              Part One for further guidance.
            </p>

            <h4 id="credential-required-set-up">Credential-Required Set-Up</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Any device that requires a user credential to be added on a
                vendor&rsquo;s external app in order to enable a key feature, or
                something required for initial set-up, is not eligible.
              </li>
              <li>
                Examples include: Matter commissioning via a cloud app, lock
                calibration via a vendor app, etc.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 id="items-that-have-missing-features-in-home-assistant">
              Items That Have Missing Features in Home Assistant
            </h4>
            <p>
              WWHA certification requires that a device&rsquo;s <strong
                >primary function</strong
              > works reliably and locally in Home Assistant. Certification will not
              be granted if the primary function does not work, regardless of how
              well secondary features perform. It will also not be granted if our
              team has no capability to test this function, or if the function is
              one of a bridge / hub.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Primary function:</strong> The core purpose of the device &mdash;
              the reason a user buys it. This includes any capability prominently
              advertised, named in the product title, shown on primary packaging/imagery,
              or reasonably expected based on the product category (e.g. a lock must
              lock; a power plug&rsquo;s on/off <em>and</em> any advertised energy
              monitoring).
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Secondary function:</strong> Additional features that support
              but aren&rsquo;t essential to the device&rsquo;s core purpose (e.g.
              a night light on a sensor, signal repeating, a physical button not tied
              to core operation).
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Determining primary vs. secondary &mdash; ask:</strong>
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Is it one of the main marketed features?</li>
              <li>
                Is it in the product name or shown prominently on the
                device/packaging?
              </li>
              <li>Is it a reason people specifically buy this product?</li>
              <li>
                Would a reasonable user feel misled if it didn&rsquo;t work?
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              If the answer to any of these is yes, treat the feature as <strong
                >primary</strong
              > &mdash; certification will not proceed if it&rsquo;s missing or not
              testable from Home Assistant, even if functional elsewhere.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Secondary feature gaps:</strong> May still qualify for certification,
              provided:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                The gap is disclosed on the device&rsquo;s Home Assistant
                integration page and, where relevant, the badge/certified device
                page.
              </li>
              <li>
                The tester confirms whether the gap is due to a <strong
                  >connectivity/protocol limitation</strong
                > (e.g. a feature not yet supported by Matter) versus a lack of vendor
                integration effort &mdash; this affects both the disclosure wording
                and improvement roadmap.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>
        </div>
      </div>
